# alloc_vecpool_2026-09-10 — allocator-call reduction, port vs C

commit:  (this change)
host:    lilith (WSL2, Ryzen 9 7950X, 23 GiB), x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
tool:    heaptrack + heaptrack_print, via tools/heaptrack_alloc_cell.sh
cell:    identity_run crop:codec-corpus/gb82-sc/windows.png 512 320 qp20 preset -1
         (screen-content still; the whole MD funnel runs, and it is small enough
         to heaptrack in seconds)
C arm:   tools/capture_c_trace/capture_c_trace.bin 512 320 20 -1 p.yuv c.obu

## WHY this cell and this metric

The user's instruction was "heaptrack vs C and fix our memory model deficits
that are amplifying allocs". The deficit is NOT peak heap — the port's peak is
a THIRD of C's. It is allocator CALL COUNT, where the port was 14.7x C:

    port  6,851,798 calls    peak heap 25.44 M
    C       466,395 calls    peak heap 69.43 M

C is low because it allocates its mode-decision working set ONCE per encoder
thread in `svt_aom_mode_decision_context_ctor` (md_process.c:214) and reuses it
for every tx-type trial of every transform unit of every block of every frame.
The port allocated the same buffers per call.

## EVERY ARM IS BYTE-IDENTICAL

Every number below was taken with the emitted bitstream hashed. All arms, and
the C reference, produce the SAME 14,871-byte OBU:

    sha256(first 16) = 00c327d57ee3fc1d

An arm whose allocation count moved but whose bytes moved too is not an
allocation result, so the hash is printed by the harness on every run.

## RESULTS

    arm                                            calls        vs base   peak heap
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    base (6a784743)                            6,851,798             --     25.44 M
    smallvec on txb_eob/cul/type + MDS3 locals 6,353,616         -7.3 %     25.44 M
    + PoolVec on the tx pipeline's outputs     3,420,176        -50.1 %     27.78 M
    + PoolVec on the MD scratch buffers        2,504,946        -63.4 %     27.69 M
    + PoolVec on cand.pred / cand.pred10       2,123,200        -69.0 %     27.38 M
    + SIZE-CLASSED free lists                  2,191,999        -68.0 %     25.57 M
    + byte-budgeted per-class depth            1,696,200        -75.2 %     25.95 M
    + the last chroma/palette scratch          1,527,403        -77.7 %     25.95 M
    + the CfL scratch (all six sites)          1,311,342        -80.9 %     25.95 M
    + palette delta-cost, walked not built     1,184,307        -82.7 %     25.95 M
    + the palette search scratch               1,172,553        -82.9 %     25.97 M
    + a real field-wise `EntropyCtx` restore    1,055,233        -84.6 %     25.99 M
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    C reference                                  466,395                    69.43 M

Excluding the `intrabc_hash` site that is AT PARITY with C (207,457 on both
sides), the port is at 847,776 against C's 258,938 — a 3.3x gap, from 26x.

Peak heap ends 0.51 M (+2.0 %) above the baseline and at 37 % of C's. The size
classes are what kept it there: with ONE undifferentiated free list the peak was
27.78 M, because a request for a block-sized buffer was served a coefficient-row
one and `realloc`ed it.

## HOW MUCH OF THE REMAINING GAP IS ACTUALLY C-SHAPED

207,457 of the port's remaining 1,527,403 are `intrabc_hash::HashTable::add`
growing its per-bucket `Vec`. C spends EXACTLY THE SAME 207,457 there --
`svt_aom_vector_setup`, reached from
`svt_aom_rtime_alloc_svt_av1_add_to_hash_map_by_row_with_precal_data`, and it is
the single largest entry in C's own profile (16.55 M peak). So that site is
already at parity and is deliberately left alone; reserving per bucket would
diverge from C's growth pattern AND multiply the table's live bytes.

Excluding it, the port is at ~1.32 M against C's ~259 K.

## WHAT WAS TRIED AND REJECTED

`SmallVec<[i32; 16]>` for `Cand::txb_q` (the per-TXB coefficient rows).
MEASURED WORSE: 6,353,616 -> 6,492,430. Inline 16 covers only a 4x4 TXB; the
8x8-and-larger majority spill to the heap AND pay a copy, because the value
stored there is MOVED out of the tx scratch today rather than copied. Reverted;
the field is pooled instead, which keeps the move. The comment on the field
records this so it is not re-attempted.

