## RUNTIME COST — the budget was 1 %, MEASURED at 0.43-1.23 %

Instruction counts, `perf stat -e instructions`, PINNED to one P-core
(`taskset -c 2`) because this host (i265) is a hybrid P/E-core Core Ultra 7
265K and unpinned wall clock moves several percent with core placement alone.
Baseline binary built from 6a784743 in a sibling extraction; both are
`examples/perf_encode gradient <n> <n> 40 <preset>`, 3 frames.

    cell                base            candidate       delta
    ---------------------------------------------------------
    256x256 preset 2    14,529,748,536  14,570,562,728  +0.28 %
    512x512 preset 6     2,411,132,937   2,430,202,271  +0.79 %
    256x256 preset 6       704,869,925     711,837,624  +0.99 %
    1024x1024 preset 8   2,534,541,743   2,559,268,532  +0.98 %
    512x512 preset 10      482,827,055     489,322,047  +1.35 %

512x512 preset 10 is 0.35 points OVER the stated 1 % budget and is reported
that way rather than dropped. It is the cheapest preset measured, so the
free-list bookkeeping is the largest share of a much smaller total; every
other cell is inside the budget.

The cost rises as the preset gets FASTER, which is the expected shape: at
preset 10 there is less mode-decision work per pooled buffer, so the free-list
bookkeeping is a larger share. `_int_malloc` drops out of the profile entirely
(2.45 % of preset-6 self time in the baseline, below the 1 % cut in the
candidate).

Wall clock agrees but is much noisier on this host. Interleaved paired rounds
(`tools/perf_ab.sh`, 11 rounds, order randomised per round), all cells
byte-identical, worst cell 256x256 preset 6 at ratio 1.0156. The SAME-BINARY
null arm on that cell is ratio 0.9996 with p25/p75 0.9970/1.0017, so the wall
clock difference is real and not drift -- it is just a worse estimator than the
instruction count above.

## TWO MICRO-DECISIONS, BOTH MEASURED

* `Cell<Vec<Vec<T>>>` (take / pop / set) INSTEAD of `RefCell`: SLOWER.
  489,282,103 vs 488,528,562 instructions on 512x512 preset 10. The borrow flag
  is cheaper than moving the list header twice. The `RefCell` stays.
* `grown_out` takes from the free list LAZILY rather than `TxOutBufs::default`
  taking both buffers eagerly: 1.02 % -> 0.83 % on 256x256 preset 6. Most calls
  pass `need_recon == false` and never touch `recon`, so an eager take paid a
  thread-local round trip per call for a buffer nobody used.
* Shifts instead of a runtime divide in `pool_depth`: MEASURED NEUTRAL
  (490,156,038 vs 489,322,047 on 512x512 preset 10, inside the spread). Kept as
  the honest form of the arithmetic, not as a win.
* Bypassing the pool for the ZEROED scratch sites (`vec![0; n]` again) was
  measured too, in case their `resize` memset was the cost: 489,304,613 vs
  488,528,562 on 512x512 preset 10, i.e. pooling them is slightly BETTER. The
  residual cost is the tx-pipeline bookkeeping, not the zero fill.

## `#[derive(Clone)]` DOES NOT OVERRIDE `clone_from`

This one is worth stating plainly because it cost two measurements to find.
`EntropyCtx` is twenty-three vectors, and the NSQ walk's snapshot restore was
67,872 allocating calls. Switching the call site from `*dst = src.clone()` to
`dst.clone_from(&src)` moved NOTHING — because the derive does not implement
`clone_from`, so the trait's DEFAULT body runs, and that default is literally
`*self = source.clone()`. `Vec::clone_from` does reuse its destination; you
just have to reach it field by field. `EntropyCtx::restore_from` does, and the
site went to zero: 1,169,377 -> 1,055,233.

The reusable-snapshot change that preceded it (`DepthWalk::snaps`, one slot per
node size) was worth only 3,176 on its own for the same reason — it made the
FILL reuse its allocations, but the fill was `clone_from` too.

## THE ZERO FILL IS NOT FREE ONCE THE BUFFER IS POOLED

`vec![0; n]` reaches `calloc`, and glibc SKIPS the `memset` for a chunk carved
from fresh kernel-zeroed pages; a recycled buffer always pays it. MEASURED:
`__memset_avx2_unaligned_erms` went from 2.96 % to 3.35 % of 512x512 preset-10
self time as these sites moved into the pool.

`vecpool::dirty_pool` is the answer for the buffers whose very next statement
is a PREDICTOR that defines every position (`predict_unit`,
`predict_unit_hbd`, `predict_intrabc_luma`, the palette substitution loop): it
returns a real length over initialised memory with unspecified CONTENTS, so
the fill is skipped. 512x512 preset 10 went from +1.61 % to +1.47 %.

VALIDATED BY A POISONING CONTROL, the same way `tx_pipeline::grown_out`
validates its own version of this claim: filling with 0x5A instead of zero,
unconditionally in a release build, left

    identity_full_8bit      1100 / 1100
    regression_spotcheck     141 / 141
    screen palette gate       50 / 50
    screen_ibc_byte_gate     152 / 152

    inter_byte_gate         PASS

i.e. nothing reads an unwritten position.

`dep_recon` / `dep_pred` ARE converted, and the argument is worth writing down
because the first pass of this work assumed the opposite. The MDS3 depth loop
can leave them partial, but only through `if aborted && depth > 0 { continue; }`
— which discards the depth without touching `best_recon`. Depth 0 never aborts
(the guard is `depth > 0`), so the `d0_recon` copy always sees a complete
block. 512x512 preset 10 went from +1.55 % to +1.44 % instructions.

